WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.158
Ice-cream sales rise. More people seek shade.

00:00:03.158 --> 00:00:06.316
Did ice cream send them into the shade?

00:00:06.316 --> 00:00:09.474
Statistical reasoning separates correlation and causation.

00:00:09.474 --> 00:00:12.632
Correlation describes how variables vary together.

00:00:12.632 --> 00:00:15.789
Causal claims need more persuasive designs.

00:00:15.789 --> 00:00:22.105
Plot the records and observe their joint trend.

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:28.421
One explanation is a direct influence.

00:00:28.421 --> 00:00:34.737
Another is a shared third influence.

00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:41.053
Hot weather could affect both.

00:00:41.053 --> 00:00:44.211
Suggesting weather does not prove it.

00:00:44.211 --> 00:00:47.368
Ask about timing, omitted factors and reverse influence.

00:00:47.368 --> 00:00:50.526
These plotted points are simulated for teaching.

00:00:50.526 --> 00:00:56.842
Look for the study’s comparisons in a news story.

00:00:56.842 --> 00:01:03.158
Ask which other explanations were tested.

00:01:03.158 --> 00:01:06.316
Suitable designs and methods can strengthen evidence.

00:01:06.316 --> 00:01:09.474
A third-factor idea is a new starting point.

00:01:09.474 --> 00:01:12.632
Keep asking so evidence can accumulate.

00:01:12.632 --> 00:01:15.789
Meet a correlation with a question before an equation.
